mynote =       {\begin{quotation}It \emph{cannot} be interpreted in terms of a transformation carrying the first into the second state, since, if both states are equilibrium states, no transformation is possible.\par In the classical elementary theory, this difficulty is circumvented by appealing to the so called ``quasi-static'' process, or a ``process slow enough to go through a sequence of equilibrium states''. Arguments as vague as these should be forbidden in any theory developed after the formulation of differential calculus.\end{quotation} p.~32. \enquote{The second law is essentially a postulate about \emph{irreversibility} of processes, since it is invariably written in the form of an inequality. Hence, thermodynamics is essentially a science dealing with irreversible processes, and it does not make sense to feel the need to call any very special branch of the science by the name of ``Thermodynamics of irreversible processes''. This misleading term arises from the fact that classical elementary thermodynamic theory only applies to system[s] which are so special as to be uncapable of undergoing any process which is not reversible \textelp{}, so that consideration of even the most elementary form of irreversibility appears as a new science.} p.~33. \enquote{Since the second law introduces a quantity which does not appear in the basic equations of thermomechanics, namely entropy, it is not needed in the solution of either the classical or the reverse problem. \textelp{} One thus has a system of equations consisting of: \iffalse(\fi\textit{a}) three balances \textelp{}. \iffalse(\fi\textit{b}) four constitutive equations \textelp{}. \iffalse(\fi\textit{c}) the second law. The fields of interest are the six ones discussed above and entropy (or alternately free energy). Hence, the second law overdetermines the problem, and must be interpreted as imposing some restrictions. Since it does not impose restrictions on the processes (as discussed before), it imposes restrictions on the constitutive equations.} \chap~3 pp.~59--60}
}

