mynote =       {\enquote{In the heat conduction case, the \textelp{Clausius-Duhem inequality} implies that \textelp{internal energy} $U$ and \textelp{entropy} $S$ do not depend on \textelp{temperature gradient} $\incr T$, while of course the heat flux $q$ does. No such simplifying uncoupling emerges in the thermodynamic theory of mixtures, as shown by M\"uller \cite{mueller1968}} p.~5. \enquote{In any physical theory, the results one obtains by excluding some effect from the start do not necessarily coincide with those one obtains by first including the effect and then allowing it to tend to zero} p.~5}
