@Book{barndorffnielsenetal1974,
editor =       {Ole E. Barndorff-Nielsen and Preben Bl{\ae}sild and Geert Schou},
title =        {Proceedings of Conference on Foundational Questions in Statistical Inference: Aarhus, May 7--12, 1973},
publisher =    {University of Aarhus},
year =         {1974},
volume =       {1},
series =       {Memoirs},
address =      {Aarhus},
keywords =     {exchangeability, Bayes, sufficient statistics, exponential family, extremal family},
mynote =       {Rasch: \begin{quotation}First a terminological remark. The ``prediction'' is suggestive of the statistician as a magician who can tell the future. Economists have an expression that is less pretentious: forecasting -- not much more reliable than weather forecasting.\par To speak seriously: you do not really \emph{predict} anything. What you do, is to calculate the distribution of the variate in question, possibly offering its mean value or the like as a likely event -- but only on the assumption that the model -- or a characteristic feature of it -- on which you based this forecasting, still holds, i.e. confronted with what eventually does happen \emph{you are faced with a test of this hypothesis} and nothing else -- \emph{you were not telling what the future would be}!\end{quotation} p.~268. Rasch: \begin{quotation}Next I wish to make it quite explicit, that the reason for using both significance and redundancy lies in the contention that \emph{every model is basically wrong}, i.e.\ it is bound to fail, given enough data.\par When you are in the possession of a set of data you may then either be in the position that your significance test tells you that the model fails, or you may not have got enough observations for that purpose. In the latter case you \emph{cannot yet reject} the model on statistical grounds -- which of course should not be construed as meaning that you really \emph{accept} it. In the former case you have to realize that the model fails -- and I have no sympathy for relaxing the significance requirement for the reason that the data are substantial enough to show it -- but that does not mean that the model is too bad to be applied in the actual case.\par To take a parallel from elementary physics: A ``mathematical pendulum'' is defined as ``a heavy point, swinging frictionless in a weightless string in vacuum''. A contraption like that was never seen; thus as a model for the motion of a real pendulum it is ``unrealistic''. Notwithstanding, it works quite well for a short time interval, but it begins soon to show a systematic decrease of the oscillation angle. To the model -- a second order differential equation -- thus requiring an amendment, a friction term is added, and now it works perfectly well for a long time, even during a few days, until another systematic deviation shows. If needed, a further correction, for air resistance, say, should be attempted -- but as a matter of fact, \emph{this is not needed}, because it has worked well enough for the purpose of the geo-physicist, which was to measure the gravity constant (``g'') with decimal places!\end{quotation} pp.~36--37. Rasch: \enquote{we should never succumb to the illusion that any of our models are correct, but we should certainly aim at \emph{making them adequate for our purposes}} p.~38}
}

